{"id":"valid-anagram","title":"Valid Anagram","difficulty":"Easy","pattern":"Arrays & Hashing","tags":["Hash Table","String","Sorting"],"companies":{"google":2,"apple":2,"meta":2,"amazon":3,"microsoft":2},"description":"Given two strings `s` and `t`, decide whether `t` is an **anagram** of `s`: the same multiset of characters arranged in any order. Return `true` or `false`.","examples":[{"input":"s = \"anagram\", t = \"nagaram\"","output":"true"},{"input":"s = \"rat\", t = \"car\"","output":"false","explanation":"`t` has a `c` that `s` lacks."},{"input":"s = \"aacc\", t = \"ccac\"","output":"false","explanation":"Same letters, but the counts differ (two `a` vs one)."}],"constraints":["0 <= s.length, t.length <= 5 * 10^4","s and t consist of lowercase English letters."],"hints":["Two strings of different length can never be anagrams. Check that first.","Anagrams have identical character counts. Count the letters of each string and compare the counts.","With a 26-letter alphabet a fixed-size array of counts is enough: add for `s`, subtract for `t`, and every slot must end at zero."],"function":"isAnagram","signature":"def isAnagram(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:","arg_types":{"s":"str","t":"str"},"return_type":"bool","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"s":"anagram","t":"nagaram"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"rat","t":"car"},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"aacc","t":"ccac"},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"","t":""},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"a","t":"ab"},"output":false},{"input":{"s":"ab","t":"ba"},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"","t":""},"output":true},{"input":{"s":"","t":""},"output":false}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def isAnagram(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:\n        if len(s) != len(t):\n            return False\n        counts = [0] * 26\n        for a, b in zip(s, t):\n            counts[ord(a) - 97] += 1\n            counts[ord(b) - 97] -= 1\n        return all(c == 0 for c in counts)\n","approach":"**Character counting.** Two strings are anagrams exactly when every character appears the same number of times in both. Walk both strings together, incrementing a counter for the letter from `s` and decrementing for the letter from `t`. If all 26 counters finish at zero the strings match.\n\n**Why it works:** the counter array is the difference of the two multisets; it is all zeros exactly when the multisets are equal. The early length check is what makes a single `zip` pass safe.\n\n**Common mistakes:** skipping the length check (then `zip` silently truncates); sorting both strings is correct but O(n log n) and interviewers usually ask for the linear version.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** What if the strings contain Unicode? (use a hash map instead of a 26-slot array). How would you find all anagrams of a pattern inside a long text? (sliding window over counts). How do you group many words by anagram class? (see Group Anagrams).","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1) (26 counters)"},"similar":["group-anagrams","permutation-in-string"],"number":5,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def isAnagram(self, s: str, t: str) -> bool:\n        pass\n"}